    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$4.27 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a plate lunch that runs $15 at any Hawaiian barbecue spot on the mainland. The gravy is flour, drippings, and broth, which is to say nearly free.",
    "shop_smart":  "Eggs are the cheapest way to dress up a bowl and add protein while you are at it. Fourteen of them cost less than one restaurant egg upcharge.\nThe gravy comes from pan drippings, butter, flour, beef broth, and a splash of soy, for pennies a bowl. A jar of premade gravy costs more and tastes worse.\nBeef is the whole bill here, so pin this recipe to the week the 4 lb pack of 93/7 goes on sale.",
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice: rinse, simmer covered with water until tender, and set aside.",
                    "Form the ground beef into 14 thin patties and season both sides with salt and black pepper.",
                    "Sear the patties in the olive oil in batches, 3 to 4 minutes per side, until browned and cooked through with no pink in the center. Set aside, leaving the drippings.",
                    "Make the gravy in the same pan: melt the butter, whisk in the flour, and cook 1 minute. Slowly whisk in the beef broth and soy sauce and simmer until it coats a spoon.",
                    "Fry the eggs in a nonstick pan until the whites are set.",
                    "Portion rice into 14 containers, add a patty, ladle gravy over, and top each with a fried egg and sliced green onions."
                ],
